A||||15||Kevin-84SE||How do I diagnose a stumble problem||||5||
Z||000000||Kevin-84SE||11-11-2000||04:16 PM||||I have what I believe is a stumble problem with my 1984 Fiero SE  original 2.5L engine wit 4 speed 4:10 manual transmission less than 49,000 miles. <BR>The problem seems to be with the transition between idle and acceleration. <BR>When I accelerate from a coast (while traveling at any speed appropriate for the currently selected gear with foot off the clutch) the car jerks (rough back and forth motion) sometimes quite hard. If I  accelerate very slowly and carefully I don't get the jerk. Once I have pushed the accelerator just a little bit, I can then push the accelerator very quickly and the car accelerates smoothly. <BR>I have no codes set in the ECM. So far I have done the following: new fuel filter, new spark plugs - wires - distributor cap - rotor, new map sensor, new throttle position sensor, new exhaust manifold and gaskets (due to cracks found in manifold), new oxygen sensor, new vapor canister, new EGR gasket, new PCV valve and PCV filter. I have checked and checked many times for vacuum leaks, I checked the operation of the Thermac, the EGR valve, Timing (it does jump around a bit, I set the average to 8 degrees on cylinders 1 and 4 with the ECM in diag mode per the label under the decklid). I cleaned the IAC which looked dirty (engine idles at about 900 RPM) and I used a decarb kit from the Fiero factory. The engine mounts seem fine. I am getting frustrated, nothing I have done has affected the problem, what should I do to diagnose this problem? <P>   Thanks,<BR>   Kevin||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||11-11-2000||06:02 PM||||Man, seems like you've hit all the bases. <P>Assuming it isn't clutch-related (glazed clutch disk or pressure plate) I'm gonna say check the inside of your distributor cap for moisture.<P>The most common way for water to get inside the cap is washing your engine, but driving in rain and even sudden temperature changes can get water in there.<P>Sounds dumb, but this can cause exactly the symptoms you describe.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||11-11-2000||08:33 PM||||Does it appear that the engine is stumbling or missing or does it feel more like driveline snap?<P>When I bought my 85 SE 4 cyl it had a lot of drive line snap when I pressed the throttle as you describe.  I installed poly bushings in the original dogbone and the car was much more responsive to throttle movement without the clunck I was experiencing.  You might want to rock the car back and forth while in grear and see how much the engine rocks around.  The poly dogbone will transmitt a lot of engine vibration to the car, I don't mind it on a manual car.  I new OEM dogbone might tighten things up.<P>If it is stumbling you might want to check fuel pressure and repalce the fuel filter if it has been in a while.  There is a small rubber hose section that connects the fuel pump to the fuel lines that can turn to mush over time.  This can cause a fuel pressure problem.<P>Good luck, keep us posted....<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-11-2000||09:13 PM||||Stumbling when you punch it is a sign of messed up timing.<P>check the MAP sensor and it's vacuum line very carefully. make sure the line is not blocked or leaking. make sure it's on the right vacuum port. The MAP is used to calculate "vacuum advance" for the ignition timing. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||11-11-2000||11:36 PM||||You might try disconnecting and plugging the vacuum line to the egr valve and see if the problem goes away.  If you are getting too much egr too soon (incorrect egr valve?), the engine could display those symptoms.  Might be worth a try.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting :)||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||11-12-2000||12:30 AM||||I would do what Rodrv6 says. I would also hook up a vacuum gauge to your engine and observe it while you drive.<P>When I first read the way you described your problem it looked to me like you had a faulty throttle position sensor that had a dead spot in its winding. I know you have replaced it but I would still check it with this simple test. Get an analog (not digital unless you have something like a fluke 88 that is designed for this test) ohmmeter and hook it up to the signal and ground wires. Disconnect the tps from the wiring harness to do this. Swipe the tps very slowly and observe the ohmmeter. The needle should move gradually without and jerks or dead spots. Make sure you move the tps in the position that you think you are having the problem. Even though the tps is new it could still be faulty. The tps is like the accelerator pump on a carburetor giving the engine the fuel enrichment it needs to accelerate before the map sensor starts to work. If the tps has a dead spot as I described than your engine will have the symptoms you described. It will stumble only in a certain throttle position and than take off. <P>I am not sure on this but those throttle position sensors may also be adjustable. If it is than all you may have to do is adjust it. <P>How is your fuel economy?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Kevin-84SE||11-12-2000||11:01 AM||||Thanks for the replies,<BR>I did find a little dirt in the distributor when I did a ohm meter test on the pickup coil documented in the service manual but no moisture that I could tell. I was thinking of replacing the pickup coil but I could not figure out how to remove it. Could the pickup coil or the ignition module cause this problem?<BR>I suspect that it is a stumble or miss more than a drivetrain problem but I am not totally sure, before I did much of the work mentioned, I took the car to a transmission specialist and he said that he thought it was a miss, he said that the transmission was fine and recommended replacing the plugs, wires, cap and rotor. after doing that performance improved some but it did not affect the stumble. When I push the car back and fourth while in gear, the car only moves a few inches and the engine does not move much at all. The dogbone looks and feels very tight. <BR>I don't have a gauge to check fuel pressure, I will have to see if I can borrow one. I have replaced the fuel filter, Sounds like I will have to drop the fuel tank to check that hose if the fuel pressure is low. <BR>The MAP sensor is connected to the correct port per the diagram on the emissions label, it is not blocked or leaking, I removed and inspected the vacuum line. I pulled the vacuum line out of the MAP sensor and I could hear the vacuum and the engine RPM's drop, put it back in and RPM's go back to normal. I verified that pulling the wire off of the MAP sensor set the proper code in the ECM, then I disconnected the battery to reset the ECM. <BR>I will try the suggestion to pull the EGR vacuum line and block it, the EGR valve is the original, I removed it and the diaphragm is not leaking and the valve is seating correctly. <BR>The 84 TPS is not adjustable, I had done the ohm meter test on the original TPS and it checked out fine but I replaced it anyway just to be sure. I am not sure on the fuel economy, I have not checked it lately and I don't want to drive it much until I get this problem fixed. <P>   Thanks, <BR>   Kevin||||reg||1
Z||000007||fldevel||11-12-2000||02:12 PM|||| bad engine mounts will do this .. the engine will jerk foreward untill somthing stops it .. the the car will jery foreward .. also check you throtle positioning senser .. that lil schitt will give you more problems then you think <BR>good luck, <BR>                               Dev out :P||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||11-12-2000||05:17 PM||||You say that the timing jumps around a bit when you put a timing light on it. The coil may be slowly going. A dying coil will misfire under a load when the demand for spark is it's greatest. Try spraying the coil with soapy water and putting the engine under a load.||||reg||1
Z||000009||strdream||11-12-2000||09:19 PM||||You gave a clue when you said that you unhooked the vac line to the map sensor and the RPMs droped. Map can be thought of as the engine load sensor. Wide open throttle makes vac drop ECM sees that and says engine is under a load and increases fuel delivery. unhooking vac line should provide a shot of air to the engine at the same time the ECM will call for more fuel. The RPMs will go UP not down.  Now why did it do that?  One of three possibilities.  1. not enough fuel pressure. Test it on the fuel rail while the stumble is happining.  2. The coil output is not adequate to produse a good spark under extreem load. Just thro one on. Its cheaper than hireing someone with a lab scope to anylize it for you. 3. The ECM is for some reason not providing the asychronous pulses to the injectors nessesary to richen the mixture enough for that much load. Before I replaced it I would want to read the data streams and compare sensor outputs to ECM inputs. Or in other words make sure it is seeing what it thinks it is seeing. could end up being resitance in sensor wires or connections.<BR>Have Fun<BR>Ross<p>[This message has been edited by strdream (edited 11-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Kevin-84SE||11-13-2000||05:28 PM||||Ross, it sounds like you may be on to something,<BR>I double checked and the RPM's definitely drop immediately after pulling the vacuum line from the MAP sensor, it almost stalls, I tried several times and a couple of times the engine did stall. after the RPM's drop, it comes back up and I see a puff of black smoke from the exhaust. I tried pulling the wire connector off instead of the vacuum line and the rpm's drop just a little, definitely not as much as when I pull the vacuum line. Would the black smoke be an indication that the coil output is not adequate under extreme load?  <P>   Thanks,<BR>   Kevin<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000011||88formula||11-14-2000||10:08 PM||||O-man. The black smoke coming out of the exhaust after you pull the map sensor vacuum line is from a rich mixture. When you pull the vacuum line to the map sensor the rpm's are supposed to go down because of the very rich mixture. It may stall because the mixture may be too rich. This is normal when you pull the vacuum line. <P>To check your map sensor connect the positive lead of a voltmeter to the B terminal (center). Connect the negative lead to ground. Keep the connector connected and just back probe through the back of the connector with the leads. Don’t pierce the insulation or you will have problems later. Turn the ignition on and the voltage should read about 4.5-5 volts. Start the engine and let it warn up. The reading should be lower than the 5 volts and should fluctuate with changes in engine rpm. If the reading does not change with engine speed than check for a vacuum signal to the sensor. If that is OK than the sensor is probably bad.<P>I don't think a map sensor will cause your problem though. If the map sensor were bad your fiero would have no power. It would start to take off and than die and at the same time the check engine light would come on and give you I think a code 33. The map sensor does the same thing that a power valve does for a carburetor.<P>You should probably take you fiero to a professional. Your problem may lie in the ignition system because you say the timing jumps around, which is not normal.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 11-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||strdream||11-14-2000||10:47 PM||||Dito to 88. I would not think the map is the problem. Pull the plug and you should be flooding it and RPMs will fall. If the vac line does not let much air into the manifold when it is unhooked it will respond the same. If it lets enough air in the RPMs go up.  When I said the coil is my first suspect that is based on your saying that you replaced all the other stuff. In general you should look to the ignition system with your symptoms.  One good thing you may have answered is the fuel pressure question. You can't make black smoke with out fuel pressure.  Does it have centrifugal advance? maybe the springs are weak and rough running will make the timing jump around. Check the dist shaft bearing too.<BR>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||11-14-2000||11:17 PM||||Ummmmmm....... MAP can be messed up and not throw codes. I've seen that more than once. It won't stall the car allot of times either.<P>Being able to wind the car up slowly ok and having it puke when you punch it is a sign of a vacuum advance problem on a "normal" mechanical distributor ignition. HEI or DIS vacuum advance is calc'd by the ECM using readings from the MAP.<P>My own car had this exact problem a few months after replacing the OE MAP. I tried a used OE MAP and the problem went away. I never got a code durring this whole thing. was annoying as hell. I've since put on a new MAP from Borg Warner and the problem hasn't come back. (The first replacement was a Wells.)||||reg||1
Z||000014||Kevin-84SE||11-15-2000||08:02 PM||||I checked again, the timing definitely jumps around, if that is not normal then I will try to check out the ignition system, I checked with a friend at my local NAPA store and he suggests replacing the pickup coil. I pulled the distributor and have been looking it over, it seems to have a light film of dirty oil covering some of the module and much of the inside of the distributor. About ¾ of the pickup coil ring has a little rust, the shaft bearing seems fine. There is one thing that I find puzzling, the spring between the gear and the bottom of the distributor has a clip that goes around the shaft bellow the spring and has three sides that come up and go into the bottom of the distributor, there is another clip that goes above the spring and it looks like the 2 clips should be connected to each other holding the spring somewhat compressed but the clips were not connected when I removed the distributor. Should they be? The service manual does not mention anything about the spring or the clips but in the diagram on page 6D-27 the spring and at least the bottom clip with the sides coming up is evident. <BR>There is a component in my distributor that is not mentioned at all in the service manual it is a red rectangular component that has one lead that connects to the + terminal on  the module and the other lead goes to ground. The device is directly underneath the connector for the +  C B connector. Anyone know what it is?<P>   Kevin||||reg||5
Z||000015||ROB MAC||11-15-2000||11:02 PM||||Look real close and check the wire from that red thing . Mine was broken , but you couldn't tell at first glance.||||reg||1
